WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
If damage to a structure wipes out a memory, was the memory stored there?

2
00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:11.623
In rabbits, a tone is paired with a puff of air to the eye.

3
00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:16.283
After training, the tone alone closes the eyelid before the puff.

4
00:00:17.043 --> 00:00:19.643
The puff itself always triggers a reflex blink.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:26.653
In 1982, Thompson's laboratory removed the lateral cerebellum on the same side as

6
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:27.653
the trained eye.

7
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:30.597
The learned response to the tone was abolished.

8
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.050
Was the eyelid simply paralysed?

9
00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.610
If so, what would happen to the reflex blink to the puff?

10
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:44.773
It would fail too.

11
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:48.893
But the reflex blink survived, so the eyelid could still move.

12
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.043
the other eye could still be conditioned.

13
00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:55.503
So the rabbits could still hear the tone, and still learn.

14
00:00:56.483 --> 00:01:01.203
With these controls, the lost response is good evidence that the cerebellum is

15
00:01:01.203 --> 00:01:02.203
needed for it.

16
00:01:02.783 --> 00:01:03.943
But needed for what?

17
00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:08.343
A lost response cannot tell a lost store from a cut output route.

18
00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:13.857
A later experiment silenced the output route with a drug, only during training.

19
00:01:14.837 --> 00:01:16.117
No learned response appeared.

20
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.697
Once the drug wore off, it was there.

21
00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:20.517
The rabbits had learned.

22
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.290
H.M.'s brain raises a second limit: which structure was needed?

23
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:31.670
After his death in 2008, it was cut into thousands of thin sections and rebuilt in

24
00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:32.670
three dimensions.

25
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:38.273
The operation had taken the front of the hippocampus, most of the amygdala, and

26
00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:40.033
nearly all the entorhinal cortex.

27
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:45.583
But the back of the hippocampus remained on both sides, with neurons still present.

28
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:51.663
The entorhinal cortex is the main cortical route into the hippocampus.

29
00:01:52.723 --> 00:01:54.643
What remained may have been largely cut off.

30
00:01:56.717 --> 00:01:58.637
The authors offered that as a model.

31
00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:03.137
Living cells in a section do not prove they were connected or working.

32
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.160
So his amnesia followed a medial temporal lesion on both sides.

33
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.560
His case alone cannot assign it to one structure.

34
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.400
Crossed failures show that the damaged tissue is needed for a task.

35
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.100
They do not tell you where memories live.
